{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794994453,"nonce":"1nXQaJf/nkaDONs/WS8zXmo9+cc=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDORPa0Bs8AAAABgUrj9g","github-url":"https://github.com/FhG-IISB/jNO/issues/208","origin":"github"},"title":"lu(backend=\"host\"): SuperLU's default ordering fills 3-D factors more than needed, and the backend falls behind in 3-D","message":"`jno.solve.lu(backend=\"host\")` factorises with SciPy's SuperLU under its default column ordering (`splu(mat)` in `jno/utils/solver/linear.py`, so `permc_spec=\"COLAMD\"`). For the structurally symmetric matrices that 3-D FEM produces, that ordering fills the factors more than a symmetric minimum-degree ordering does, and the backend falls behind the others quickly as a 3-D problem grows.\n\nMeasured on `main` (b3dcdd22); the default ordering is the same on release 0.4.2.\n\n## Measurement: fill of the factors\n\nP2 elasticity on the unit cube, all faces clamped; `nnz(L) + nnz(U)` of `scipy.sparse.linalg.splu` on the assembled operator.\n\n```python\nimport jax\njax.config.update(\"jax_enable_x64\", True)\nimport jax.numpy as jnp\nimport numpy as np\nimport scipy.sparse as sp\nimport scipy.sparse.linalg as spla\nimport jno\n\ninner, symgrad, trace = jno.np.inner, jno.np.symgrad, jno.np.trace\nfor h in (0.18, 0.125, 0.09):\n    d = jno.shape.box(0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1, size=h).domain()\n    u, phi = d.fem_symbols(value_shape=(3,), order=2)\n    X = d.variable(\"interior\", split=True)[:3]\n    eps = lambda w: symgrad(w, X)\n    f = jnp.array([0.0, 0.0, -1.0])\n    fem = jno.fem([\n        0.5 * trace(eps(u)) * trace(eps(phi)) + inner(eps(u), eps(phi), n_contract=2) - inner(f, phi.bind(x=X[0], y=X[1], z=X[2]), n_contract=1),\n        u(*d.variable(\"boundary\", split=True)[:3]) - (0.0, 0.0, 0.0),\n    ])\n    A, b = fem.operator\n    A = sp.coo_matrix((np.asarray(A.data), tuple(np.asarray(A.indices).T)), shape=A.shape).tocsc()\n    fill = {spec: (lambda lu: lu.L.nnz + lu.U.nnz)(spla.splu(A, permc_spec=spec)) for spec in (\"COLAMD\", \"MMD_AT_PLUS_A\")}\n    print(f\"unknowns {A.shape[0]}, nnz(A) {A.nnz}: COLAMD {fill['COLAMD']}, MMD_AT_PLUS_A {fill['MMD_AT_PLUS_A']}, ratio {fill['COLAMD'] / fill['MMD_AT_PLUS_A']:.1f}\")\n```\n\n| Unknowns | nnz(A) | nnz(L + U), COLAMD | nnz(L + U), MMD_AT_PLUS_A | Ratio |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 6,360 | 185,396 | 1,750,128 | 1,042,024 | 1.7 |\n| 13,116 | 473,216 | 6,290,422 | 4,182,472 | 1.5 |\n| 36,648 | 1,742,070 | 48,355,026 | 29,534,778 | 1.6 |\n\nSo the default ordering stores about 1.6 times the factor entries that `MMD_AT_PLUS_A` needs on this operator, at every size.\n\n## Second observation, not explained by the fill\n\nIn a loop that re-solves the same problem with a rescaled per-cell coefficient (one compiled program, the factorisation redone on every call), the host backend needed more time per call and a higher peak of resident memory than `backend=\"pardiso\"` and `backend=\"cudss\"` on the same problem, and the gap widened with size on three 3-D problems (P2 elasticity, P2 neo-Hookean, Taylor-Hood Stokes; 9,000 to 81,000 unknowns). This was measured on release 0.4.2 and not repeated on `main`. The peak of resident memory was several times what the factor counts above account for. Where the rest goes (copies of the factors, a factorisation kept per call, the transpose for the adjoint) was not investigated.\n\n## Requests\n\n- Pass `permc_spec=\"MMD_AT_PLUS_A\"` when the pattern is structurally symmetric, which jNO already detects for the other backends.\n- Find what the host backend keeps beyond one set of factors in a re-solve loop.\n- Say in the solver guide that `backend=\"host\"` is the fallback for 3-D problems of this size, and name the backends that are meant for them.","files":null}]}